---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';
import ConvertDoor from '../components/ConvertDoor.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why is the JPG so much smaller?',
    a: 'PNG writes every pixel exactly, which is heavy for photos. JPG writes a close approximation and throws the difference away, which is light. For a photo or a screenshot, the saving is usually large; for a logo with flat colours and sharp edges, look at the result closely, because those edges are where JPG fuzz shows first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to the transparent parts?',
    a: 'JPG cannot hold see-through pixels at all. Any transparent part of your PNG is painted over a colour you pick at the tray (white unless you change it), and the result line says when this happened. If the transparency is the point of the picture, keep it a PNG, or use the PNG to WebP door, which keeps it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What quality should I pick?',
    a: '90 unless you have a reason. It keeps photos looking like themselves at a fraction of the PNG\'s size. Lower trades detail for bytes; the exact-KB tool next door exists when a door sets a hard size limit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my file go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is decoded, re-encoded and offered back to you inside this page. Nothing is uploaded.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to convert a PNG to a JPG",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick a PNG", text: "Drop a PNG on the tray, or click to pick one. It stays on this device. A file in the wrong format is pointed at the right door." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Set the conversion", text: "Pick the quality, and if your PNG has see-through parts, the colour they are painted over: JPG cannot hold transparency, and the colour is white unless you change it." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Convert to JPG", text: "Press 'Convert to JPG'. The PNG is decoded and written as a JPG here in the page; the result line prints the bytes before and after." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: "Download the JPG", text: "Download the JPG at the dimensions and size the result line states." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="PNG to JPG: convert a PNG to a JPG, free, in your browser · Benchzy" description="Convert one PNG to a JPG inside your browser: smaller files for forms, shops and sites that want JPG. Transparent parts are painted over a colour you pick, stated before the run. No upload, no account.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · convert</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">PNG to JPG</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        A PNG is an exact, heavy file. Forms, shops and older sites often want a JPG, or the
        PNG is simply too big to send. This door converts one PNG to a JPG, here in your
        browser. The one real change is stated before you run: PNG can be see-through and JPG
        cannot, so transparent parts are painted over a colour you pick (white unless you
        change it). A whole folder at once is the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/batch-convert-images/">batch convert page</a>;
        the reverse trip is <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/jpg-to-png/">JPG to PNG</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For one PNG that has to arrive as a JPG: a screenshot for a form, a product photo for a shop, a picture for a site that refuses PNG." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick a PNG' }, { n: '02', t: 'Set quality and the background colour' }, { n: '03', t: 'Download the JPG' }]} />

    <ConvertDoor from="png" to="jpeg" note="The format changes, and the dimensions do not. A photo usually lands far under its PNG size. If your PNG has see-through parts, they are painted over the colour below, because JPG cannot hold transparency; the result line says when that happened." />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The PNG is decoded by the open-source jSquash codecs, any transparency is painted
          over your colour, and the picture is written as a JPG at your quality, the same
          engine the batch convert page runs. The first run loads the image program (about
          2 MB, one time only). The download is how we protect your privacy: the program
          comes to your device and does the work there, so your file never leaves it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          JPG is lossy: what it throws away does not come back, so keep the PNG if it is your
          master copy. Sharp edges on flat colours (logos, text in screenshots) are where the
          loss shows first; check the preview on the result. Converting a JPG-sized problem
          is not this door's job either: if a door sets a hard KB limit, the
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/compress-image/">exact-KB tool</a> lands
          on the number itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/convert-image.js';
  </script>
</Base>
